EXT. SALT FLATS - DUSK

Blinding white ground. A lone wagon with a broken wheel. Heat shimmer.

AMOS QUARLES (50s, sunburned, hat brim torn) sits on the tailgate with a pencil stub and a crumpled sheet of paper. He writes. Stops. Rereads.

AMOS (V.O.)
Dear Ruth. The wheel went at noon. The water went at two.

He licks the pencil.

AMOS (V.O.) (CONT'D)
I walked to the ridge. Nothing there but wind.

He stands. Shades his eyes. Nothing in any direction.

Amos kicks the dead wheel. It rolls an inch, falls over.

AMOS (V.O.) (CONT'D)
I think I'll wait for the stars. They never lied to me yet.

He sits back down. Folds the letter into his hat.

Far off, a faint metallic ring. A bell.

Amos looks up.

AMOS
(whispering)
Well, now.

He stands. Picks up his canteen. Shakes it. Empty. He walks toward the sound anyway.
